## Architecture

```mermaid
graph TB
    U[User / Browser] -->|district + tier + messages| RATE[slowapi Rate Limiter]
    RATE --> CHAT["/chat — Chat Agent"]
    RATE --> GCHAT["/api/gemini-chat — PDF Analysis"]
    RATE --> ECHAT["/api/election-chat — Results Agent"]

    CHAT --> BDC["build_database_context()"]
    BDC --> LN[Legislator narrative<br/>token-scoring disambiguation]
    BDC --> CF[Campaign finance<br/>VA SBE Schedule A]
    BDC --> PAC["PAC–vote correlation<br/>dual-gate + inference rule"]
    BDC --> FED[Federal roll-call votes]
    BDC --> GAP[Gap signals<br/>zero_records / out_of_scope]

    LN & CF & PAC & FED --> DB[("polls.db<br/>SQLite 2.2GB · 76 tables")]

    CHAT -->|query embedding| VOYAGE[Voyage AI<br/>voyage-law-2]
    VOYAGE --> CHROMA[("ChromaDB<br/>bill text · speeches · news")]
    CHROMA --> COHERE[Cohere rerank-english-v3.0]
    COHERE <-->|cache hit/miss| RCACHE[(Rerank cache)]

    BDC -->|assembled SQL context| CHAT
    CHAT -->|free| SONNET[Claude Sonnet 4.6]
    CHAT -->|paid| OPUS[Claude Opus 4.8]
    SONNET & OPUS --> CAUS["Causation guard<br/>banned-phrase regex"]
    CAUS -->|safe response| U

    style PAC fill:#ff6b6b,color:#fff
    style CAUS fill:#ffa94d,color:#000
    style GAP fill:#74c0fc,color:#000
    style DB fill:#51cf66,color:#000
```

**Request flow:** query arrives with district/tier metadata → rate limiter → `build_database_context()` runs keyword-gated SQL queries and assembles verified, sourced context blocks (inserting gap signals where data is absent) → Voyage embeds the query, ChromaDB retrieves candidate documents, Cohere reranks them (cache-first) → full context goes to the tier-appropriate model → output passes a causation safety check → sourced response returned.

---

## How grounding works

The model is a **synthesizer, not a source.** Before each response, the context builder hands the LLM verified rows like this:

```text
[Database Context — federal vote lookup]
Source: Congress.gov / House Clerk roll-call tables
target_name=Jennifer A. Kiggans; bioguide_id=K000399
congress_votes summary: congress=119; total_votes=550; yea_votes=409; ...
source_url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2026/0200
```

It does **not** receive *"tell me what you know about Kiggans's record."* If no row matches, the builder injects an explicit gap signal instead of empty context:

```text
[Database Context — va_bills HB999]
lookup_status=zero_records
detail=No record found for HB999 in session 2026.
Do NOT fabricate a vote or outcome for this bill.
```

A newsroom that publishes *"Senator X voted Yes on HB 123"* needs that to be true. An LLM confabulating from approximate embeddings is a liability — so embeddings are **supplemental** (bill text, speech excerpts, news) and never override a SQL-sourced count, vote, or dollar figure.

---

## The PAC–vote correlation layer

The most carefully engineered component surfaces the relationship between PAC money and votes **without ever asserting causation** — a claim no public record can support. Three independent defenses:

1. **Dual-gate entry** — the function only runs when the query contains *both* PAC vocabulary *and* vote vocabulary.
2. **In-context inference rule** — every assembled block closes with an explicit instruction that the data shows correlation only.
3. **Output-time guard** — a compiled regex of forbidden causal connectors checks the generated text before it ever reaches the client.

The result: *"PAC money bought this vote"* is **structurally impossible to generate**, not merely discouraged.

---

## Tech stack

**AI / retrieval** — Anthropic Claude (Sonnet 4.6 / Opus 4.8), Google Gemini 2.5 Flash, Voyage AI (`voyage-law-2`), Cohere Rerank (`rerank-english-v3.0`), ChromaDB
**API** — FastAPI, Gunicorn + Uvicorn (2 workers), Pydantic, SlowAPI rate limiting
**Geospatial** — GeoPandas (district lookups), Folium (interactive choropleths)
**Data** — SQLite (single 2.2 GB file, no ORM), Render persistent disk
**Infra** — Render Standard, weekly cron ingestion, env-var model pinning, autoDeploy on `main`

---

## Data assets

| Asset | Volume | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Virginia roll-call votes | ~1.3M votes, 25 years | OpenStates / LegiScan, VA SBE |
| Campaign contributions (Schedule A) | 2,212,860 records | Virginia SBE |
| Campaign finance reports | 213,942 records | Virginia SBE |
| Committee testimony | 150,904 records | Legislative proxy data |
| Congressional floor statements | 110,025 records | Congress.gov / House Clerk |
| FEC independent expenditures | Per-cycle | FEC.gov Schedule E |
| VA lobbyist registry | Annual | DPOR |
| Legislator civic profiles | 168 pre-generated narratives | Derived from DB |
| Election results | 2018–2025 | Virginia SBE |

---

## Engineering decisions & tradeoffs

A few choices that show the reasoning, not just the result:

- **SQL-first, RAG-supplemental.** More complex retrieval logic in exchange for never confabulating a vote count. Accuracy beats apparent fluency.
- **Explicit decline over silent gaps.** *"I don't know"* feels less impressive than a confident wrong answer — and is exactly why newsrooms trust it.
- **Single-file SQLite over Postgres.** Data is read-heavy with weekly ingestion, so concurrent writes aren't needed. Gains: zero connection overhead, trivial backup, and it runs on the cheapest tier with a persistent disk.
- **Pre-generated legislator narratives.** Trades occasional staleness for cutting a 30–60s live-generation step off every legislator query.
- **Memory-bounded ingestion.** Render Standard caps at 512 MB and the stack already loads `torch`, so weekly ingestion runs *inside the web container* via an admin endpoint rather than spawning an OOM-prone subprocess at startup.

---

## Deployment

Deployed on **Render Standard** behind 2 Uvicorn workers with a 120s timeout (data-rich Opus responses can take 60–90s). A 2.2 GB SQLite file lives on a persistent disk seeded once at build time, not rebuilt per deploy. A weekly cron job ingests fresh congressional data by calling an admin endpoint inside the container that owns the disk — cron jobs can't mount disks directly.

---

## Roadmap

VoteIQ is one product in a broader civic-data ecosystem. Near-term: a Mapbox choropleth layer, expanded news grounding, and notification delivery. The differentiator is **local-government coverage** — city-council votes, municipal finance, and zoning — data no standardized national tool currently surfaces.

---

## About

Built solo, from scratch, by a developer with a GIS background — combining spatial analysis, retrieval architecture, and production AI engineering into a platform real newsrooms can rely on. The guiding principle: **reverse the direction of surveillance** — use AI to make public data accessible to citizens, rather than harvesting personal data from them.



*Every claim traceable to a public record.*

